  "title": "Repeated Dosing",
  "body": " Repeated Dosing    The model in Part 1 describes what happens after treatment stops. But before treatment stops, medication is usually taken on a schedule. A simple way to model repeated dosing is to update the concentration at regular time intervals.  Suppose a dose is taken every days. Between doses, the medication concentration decays exponentially. If the elimination constant is , then after days, the concentration is multiplied by Then a new dose increases the concentration by an amount .      Let be the medication concentration immediately after the th dose.  Explain why the concentration immediately after the next dose satisfies   In this model, what does each term represent?      Suppose the half-life is days and a dose is taken every days.  First compute   and then compute   Interpret the number in words.      For simplicity, suppose and . Use the recurrence   to compute . Round your answers to three decimal places.                                A steady state for the recurrence   is a value such that if , then .  Find a formula for in terms of and .      The recurrence   is not a linear transformation because of the added constant . However, it can be written as a matrix update by adding an extra coordinate:   Verify that this matrix equation gives the same recurrence.      Use the Sage cell below to explore the repeated dosing model . The horizontal axis counts doses or dosing intervals. This is a simplified model that does not include the continuous decay between doses, but it captures the overall pattern of how concentration changes with repeated dosing.   Try changing . What happens when is close to ? What happens when is close to ?      Use the Sage cell below to explore the repeated dosing model with exponential decay between doses. The curve decays continuously between dose times, then jumps upward when a new dose is taken.   Try increasing . What changes about the decay between doses? Try increasing . What changes about the gap between the peak just after a dose and the trough just before the next dose?      In this model, regular dosing does not simply keep the concentration constant. Instead, the concentration moves toward a steady pattern determined by the dose size, dosing interval, and half-life.  Explain why this matters when thinking about interruptions to medication access. What information would be missing if we only knew whether someone had taken a dose recently?    "
