  "body": " Interacting Concentrations    So far, we have modeled one medication concentration at a time. But many biological systems involve feedback. For example, one hormone level can influence the production or suppression of another.  We now study a simplified two-variable model. Let represent an estradiol-related concentration and let represent a testosterone-related concentration.      Consider the system   Here are positive constants, and represents a steady external input.  Interpret each term in the model:             Use the Sage cell below to explore how the solutions change when the parameters change. The graph shows and over time after treatment is interrupted.   Try increasing and decreasing and . How do the graphs change? How does this match the eigenvalues and ?      Rewrite the system   in matrix form as   where   Identify the matrix and the vector .      An equilibrium occurs when and .  Solve the system   to find the equilibrium point .      Suppose treatment is interrupted, so the external input changes from to .  The new system is   Find the new equilibrium point.      To make the system concrete, suppose   First assume regular treatment with . Find the equilibrium .  Then assume treatment is interrupted, so . Find the new equilibrium.      The matrix for the homogeneous part of the system is   Because this matrix is triangular, its eigenvalues are visible on the diagonal.  What are the eigenvalues of ? What do they tell us about whether the system moves toward equilibrium or away from it?      The model in this part is simplified: it uses only two variables, assumes constant parameters, and represents feedback with a linear term. Real endocrine systems are more complex.  Still, the model shows that interrupting one input can affect more than one quantity. Explain how this changes the interpretation of a phrase like the medication leaves the body after a few half-lives.     "
