  "body": " PCA and -SNE by Hand: Two Toy Examples    Consider the dataset consisting of four points:    Plot the four points in the plane.  Describe the geometric relationship between and .  Do the points appear to lie approximately along a line?       Compute the mean of each coordinate:   Then subtract the mean from each point to obtain a centered dataset.  Verify that the centered points are:       Form the centered data matrix . Then compute the covariance matrix.      Recall that the covariance matrix   describes how the dataset spreads in different directions.   What are the variances of and ?  Which variable varies more?  Is the covariance positive or negative?  What does this tell you about how and change together?       Based on the covariance matrix, predict the direction of the first principal component.  Is the dominant direction closer to horizontal, vertical, or diagonal?  Explain your reasoning without computing eigenvectors.        PCA identifies directions in which the dataset varies the most.  Plot the first principal component direction on the same graph as the data points.  Project the data points onto the first principal component direction and plot the projected points on a number line.  How much of the variance does the first principal component account for?        Compare this dataset to the example in the notes in which the covariance matrix has equal diagonal entries.    Which dataset has a stronger preferred direction of variation?    Why does PCA reduce this dataset effectively to one dimension?        Consider the following six points in the plane:    Plot these six points.   Which points appear close together?  Which points appear far apart?  How many clusters do you see?       Compute the squared distances from point to each of the other points.  That is, compute:   Which distances are small? Which are large?      In -SNE, similarities are converted into probabilities using    Explain why and should be large.  Explain why , , and should be very small.       Repeat the same reasoning for point .   Which points are most likely neighbors of ?  Which points are unlikely to be neighbors of ?       A -SNE embedding tries to preserve neighbor relationships rather than exact distances.   Sketch a possible two-dimensional arrangement of the six points that preserves these neighbor relationships.  Should the distance between clusters be interpreted precisely?  Should distances within clusters be interpreted precisely?       Compare how PCA and -SNE would treat this dataset.   Would PCA primarily detect a direction of variation or a clustering structure?  Why is this dataset better suited to -SNE than PCA?     "
